#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1552ced-b2c9-56e1-9d95-9d1a59cab06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303716} {:answer/id #uuid "542f6be2-8c2a-5816-959a-01278e6f8720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303717} {:answer/id #uuid "a45f1826-9181-53e6-816e-ed1c1f38cb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303718} {:answer/id #uuid "62e50f6b-6b56-5970-9cb5-c4dfdcb2678b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303719} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fac620-ec34-5793-8587-67c80fe8f909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a45f1826-9181-53e6-816e-ed1c1f38cb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303718} :answer-field/id #uuid "f79a16b8-7f2e-5f88-9ef5-c49a8f45e94d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b542e7-13bb-5855-a1b4-10e9a704f0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113763" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+3x-2,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x+3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(x+1)^{2}=0\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2+1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0+1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=-1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=-1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439e25f7-5d47-57e7-bae8-6b921ff83669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303723} {:answer/id #uuid "07522304-f2ac-58fe-ad99-ef8d85bbc088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303724} {:answer/id #uuid "62e6e85b-ac53-521d-a8ee-e27f130d65a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725} {:answer/id #uuid "baf6a1c6-6d62-5136-bf55-eb7242a42ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303726} {:answer/id #uuid "4779cb38-451c-50cb-b14a-57526c3a2a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf6a1c6-6d62-5136-bf55-eb7242a42ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303726} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad9f6ea-5b1a-500e-ab5e-07129d025c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85c5d136-bff0-5d6f-b8b5-5a061746fd98" :question/math-academy-id "q-113765" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=7-{x}^{7},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{6}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-7(-1)^{6}=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-7(1)^{6}=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d52ba4-6bf3-5ff5-ad6b-26d5a3d49268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102910} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc84b31-210f-5272-b5ac-aa13c34450de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102911} {:answer/id #uuid "e291d489-7df2-5f09-be2f-670ee85d35b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102912} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0f2bde-2897-52fb-ab91-d2748f3f2531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102913} {:answer/id #uuid "2e731b16-1386-5fa8-b27c-421905b5f3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b0f2bde-2897-52fb-ab91-d2748f3f2531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102913} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc126653-c370-562c-b55d-f4e4dc8bcb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53c65332-08ea-53f5-952a-39779d99b5b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-21465" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}=-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-4(-1)^{2}(-1-\\frac{3}{2})=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{2}(1-\\frac{3}{2})=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)^{2}(2-\\frac{3}{2})=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b9de64c-9838-55a8-9ef6-fc47990c9621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303730} {:answer/id #uuid "c59833bb-6c9a-5fa1-b388-9fe96c8732ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303731} {:answer/id #uuid "5a54aba1-813a-58f8-b4e7-4e3d46c7fe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303732} {:answer/id #uuid "be3b8759-5f1c-5bcd-bf4b-26de86634f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303733} {:answer/id #uuid "69f1cf46-1e79-5193-8eb7-d5d0fb283371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59833bb-6c9a-5fa1-b388-9fe96c8732ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303731} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dcbf80f-5823-5249-bb0f-d4b6d38fa677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31ae5e63-813a-51de-aa8d-389cb224f1d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-22754" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=2$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+2)-(x-1)\\cdot (2x-2)}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2-2{x}^{2}+4x-2}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ and $x=2$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{-(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}{((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-(1)^{2}+2(1)}{((1)^{2}-2(1)+2)^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{-(3)^{2}+2(3)}{((3)^{2}-2(3)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6cde382-2326-5e06-b5fb-04ce4c30ead2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6cde382-2326-5e06-b5fb-04ce4c30ead2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303737} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eecc1d2-90cc-551c-a730-ad4009139afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5607589-4cc2-5871-a40a-4c3842dcb8f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-314706" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2(x-1){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x-1+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-3)(-1)=3{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}(-1)(1)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & ={e}^{2}(1)(3)=3{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1.$"}]}
